OSKit Arch -- x86_64 CPU Primitives

Architecture-specific CPU operations for x86_64: interrupt
enable/disable, and thread stack frame construction.

cli/sti are extern (provided by boot.s as bare instruction
+ ret) to avoid Sysl function prologue/epilogue overhead.

    module oskit.arch

Offset from saved SSP to the return-value register (RAX)
in the context frame. Used by syscall_return to write the
result where restore_context will pop it into RAX.
x86_64 layout: R15(0)..RBX(104)..RAX(112)..RIP(120)..

    val SYSCALL_RETURN_OFFSET = 112
    val STACK_FRAME_SIZE = 160       // 20 fields × 8 bytes (15 GP regs + 5 iretq)

Disable interrupts (x86 cli instruction).

    extern arch_cli()

Enable interrupts (x86 sti instruction).

    extern arch_sti()

Set x86 hardware watchpoint on a 4-byte address (uses DR0).
CPU raises #DB on any write to the watched address.

    extern set_hw_watchpoint(addr: u64)

Save and restore IRQ state for nestable critical sections.
arch_irq_save reads RFLAGS, disables interrupts (cli), and
returns the previous flags. arch_irq_restore writes the
saved flags back, which re-enables interrupts iff IF was
set when the save happened. Provided by stubs.c so the
inline asm doesn't fight the Sysl prologue.

    extern arch_irq_save() -> u64
    extern arch_irq_restore(prev: u64)

Spinlock acquire. Spins using lock cmpxchg until the lock
is obtained, then disables interrupts. Lock is 0=free, 1=held.
x86 calling convention: rdi = first arg (lock pointer).

    arch_spinlock_acquire(lock: *i64)
        asm("movq $$1, %rsi\n.spin_acq:\nxorq %rax, %rax\nlock cmpxchgq %rsi, (%rdi)\njnz .spin_acq\ncli")

Spinlock release. Enables interrupts, then stores 0 to lock.

    arch_spinlock_release(lock: *i64)
        asm("sti\nmovq $$0, (%rdi)")

Update TSS.RSP0 for the next ring 3 thread. Called by the
scheduler on context switch. Provided by boot.s.

    extern tss_set_rsp0(rsp0: i64)

Build a fake interrupt frame on the supervisor stack so
that restore_context in boot.s can pop it and iretq into
the new thread. x86_64 layout (20 fields, bottom to top):

15 GP regs: R15..R14..R13..R12..R11..R10..R9..R8..RBP..RDI..RSI..RDX..RCX..RBX..RAX
iretq frame: RIP, CS, RFLAGS, RSP, SS

The thread's user stack gets thread_exit as a return address
so that a thread function returning normally lands in the
exit trampoline.

    extern thread_exit: byte

    build_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        // Place thread_exit as return address on user stack
        val ret_sp = usp - 8
        *(*i64(ret_sp)) = i64(&thread_exit)

        var sp = ssp
        // iretq frame (what the CPU would push on interrupt)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x10)         // SS  (kernel data segment)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = ret_sp            // RSP (user stack, with return addr)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x202)        // RFLAGS (IF=1, reserved bit 1)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x08)         // CS  (kernel code segment)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = entry_point       // RIP
        // 15 GP registers (all zero)
        var i = 0
        while i < 15
            sp -= 8
            *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0)
            i += 1
        sp

Build a ring 3 stack frame on the per-process kernel stack.
Used by create_process_suspended for user processes. The frame
is built directly on the kernel stack (identity-mapped), not
in the child's address space. Does NOT write thread_exit to
usp — the caller handles that via vm_copy_to.

On iretq, the CPU detects CS RPL=3 and switches to ring 3,
loading SS:RSP from the frame (user stack).

    build_user_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        var sp = ssp
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x23)         // SS  (user data, RPL=3)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = usp               // RSP (user stack) — points at SysV
                                        // init frame that PM built at `usp`.
                                        // aarch64 sets SP_EL0 = usp similarly.
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x202)        // RFLAGS (IF=1)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x1B)         // CS  (user code, RPL=3)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = entry_point       // RIP
        var i = 0
        while i < 15
            sp -= 8
            *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0)
            i += 1
        sp

Set up the initial context frame for a new process. On x86_64,
the context frame is built directly on the per-process kernel
stack (identity-mapped). The CPU switches to this stack via
TSS.RSP0 on ring 3 to ring 0 transitions. Returns the SSP value.

    arch_setup_process_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64, kstack_top_val: i64, ptbr_val: u64) -> i64
        build_user_stack_frame(entry_point, usp, kstack_top_val)
        kstack_top_val - STACK_FRAME_SIZE
